   "text": {
    "L1": "A pinhole camera is a closed box with a tiny hole and a translucent screen.",
    "L2": "Light travels in straight lines, so rays cross at the pinhole and make a small, inverted image.",
    "L3": "Moving the screen nearer makes the image smaller. A bigger hole makes it brighter but blurred.",
    "L4": "That busts the myth: bigger does not mean sharper, because overlapping images blur.",
    "L5": "A periscope uses two plane mirrors facing each other, each tilted at 45 degrees to the tube.",
    "L6": "Light reflects down from the top mirror, then into the eye, so you can see over a wall.",
    "L7": "A kaleidoscope uses mirror strips at 60 degrees; repeated reflections make a symmetrical pattern.",
    "L8": "Now choose the image made by a pinhole camera, and never look at the Sun through one."
   },
